GUI modernization, Phase 4 (second slice): the 49-kind event catalogue
(ADR 0004) - generated source of truth, hand-written bilingual labels, a
completeness test on each side of the generation boundary, and real
frontend consumption in IncidentCard.tsx and Diagnostics.tsx (the two
call sites that rendered a bare kind or family code with no human name).

## 1. Why a generator, not a hand-maintained list

internal/event/kinds.go declares exactly 49 `Kind` constants across 10
families (confirmed by reading the file in full, not assumed from an
earlier count): link=5, l2=7, l3=8, flow=6, dhcp=5, dns=3, policy=2,
metric=1, change=3, system=9. ADR 0004 requires "a new Go kind cannot
silently ship without a matching GUI entry" - a hand-maintained TS list
would drift the same way docs/schema.md's table once did (the reserved
comment in internal/event/coverage_test.go: "Twenty-five kinds were once
declared and produced by nothing at all"). This project already has the
fix for that failure mode - internal/event/coverage_test.go's
`declaredKinds`/`repoRoot`, which parse kinds.go's AST rather than
trusting a second list - so the catalogue reuses the same technique
instead of inventing a different one.

## 2. What was built

- `internal/event/gen/main.go` (new): a standalone generator, run with
  `go run ./internal/event/gen`, matching the existing `ai/eval/gen`
  convention (manually run, documented, not wired into the Makefile).
  Parses kinds.go's AST for every `Kind`-typed string constant and the
  `Families` var's literal, writes sorted JSON to
  `desktop/src/i18n/generated/kinds.json`.
- `internal/event/kind_catalogue_test.go` (new):
  `TestTheGeneratedKindCatalogueIsUpToDate` re-parses kinds.go directly
  (reusing `declaredKinds`/`repoRoot` from coverage_test.go, adding
  `declaredFamilies` alongside them) and fails if the checked-in
  kinds.json does not match byte-for-byte in content - the same
  "re-derive from source, do not trust the second copy" shape as
  `TestTheSchemaDocumentListsEveryKind`.
- `desktop/src/i18n/kindCatalogue.ts` (new): hand-written `{en, ar}`
  labels for all 49 kinds and all 10 families, plus `labelForKind`/
  `labelForFamily` returning `{name, known}` - `known: false` is the
  ADR's "unknown or newer localization key falls back to the original,
  never a crash, never blank text" path for a kind this build predates.
- `desktop/src/i18n/kindCatalogue.test.ts` (new): checks kindCatalogue's
  key set against the generated JSON (missing/extra both fail), checks
  every label is non-empty in both languages and is not just the raw
  code again, and checks `labelForKind`/`labelForFamily`'s known/unknown
  behaviour directly.
- `desktop/src/components/IncidentCard.tsx`: the two call sites that
  rendered a bare `Kind` string (`link.kind` in the chain, and
  `incident.root_cause.kind`) now render the human name (bold) followed
  by the technical code in `TechnicalValue` - the component Phase 2
  built for exactly this "technical value stays visible, isolated LTR"
  purpose. A `KindName` helper covers both call sites so the "don't
  print the fallback code twice" logic exists once.
- `desktop/src/pages/Diagnostics.tsx`: the "events by family" breakdown
  (`byFamily.map`) had the same gap - raw `link`/`l2`/`l3`/... codes with
  no name in either language. Same treatment via `labelForFamily`.

## 3. Proving the generator and both completeness tests actually catch drift

Not just "it compiled and passed once" - each guard was deliberately
broken and confirmed to fail, then restored:

    $ go run ./internal/event/gen
    wrote 49 kinds and 10 families to .../desktop/src/i18n/generated/kinds.json

    $ <removed the last kind and last family from the generated JSON by script>
    $ go test ./internal/event/... -run TestTheGeneratedKindCatalogueIsUpToDate -v
    --- FAIL: TestTheGeneratedKindCatalogueIsUpToDate
        kind_catalogue_test.go:57: .../kinds.json's kinds are stale against
        kinds.go - run `go run ./internal/event/gen`
        kind_catalogue_test.go:61: .../kinds.json's families are stale ...

    $ go run ./internal/event/gen   # restored
    $ go test ./internal/event/... -run TestTheGeneratedKindCatalogueIsUpToDate -v
    --- PASS: TestTheGeneratedKindCatalogueIsUpToDate

    $ <deleted the system.update_failed entry from kindCatalogue.ts>
    $ npx vitest run src/i18n/kindCatalogue.test.ts
    FAIL  ... gives every generated kind a non-empty label in both languages
    AssertionError: system.update_failed has no entry: expected undefined to be defined
    2 failed | 5 passed (7)

    $ <restored kindCatalogue.ts, confirmed via grep -c the entry is back exactly once>
    $ npx vitest run src/i18n/kindCatalogue.test.ts
    Test Files  1 passed (1)
         Tests  7 passed (7)

## 4. Arabic content - reused established vocabulary, did not invent a second one

Before writing any label, grepped rules/*.yaml's already-reviewed `i18n.ar`
blocks (Phase 4's first slice) for the same concepts, so the same fact
reads the same way in a rule's `why` and in a raw event kind:
المسار (route), العنوان (address), عنوان العتاد (MAC), البوابة (gateway),
الواجهة (interface), المُحلِّل (DNS resolver), توقف ... عن الإجابة
("stopped answering" - the established idiom, used instead of a literal
calque of "unreachable" for `l3.icmp_unreachable`).

Cross-checked the execution order's own glossary table before writing
"collector": it names «جامعة/جامعات» as the wrong word already live at
`wizard_permissions_collectors` and prescribes «وحدة جمع البيانات» - used
that, not the wrong word, for `system.collector_down`. Checked
`translations.ts` before reusing "حزمة" (already claimed for "evidence
bundle" throughout the app, `evidence_title` etc.) - used the distinct
established networking term «رزمة» for a packet in `policy.drop_burst`
instead of overloading a word this app already uses for something else.

