## 6. Actually looked at it - not just the test suite

Launched the Vite dev server, opened the demo recording in the browser,
and read every incident the demo produces in both languages via the
rendered DOM text (`get_page_text`), not a screenshot alone. Every kind
that appears in this real dataset - `link.down`, `link.up`,
`l2.neighbor_failed`, `l2.arp_binding_changed`, `l2.duplicate_ip`,
`dhcp.server_seen`, `dns.resolver_changed`, `flow.first_failure_for_pair`,
`flow.handshake_fail`, `l3.route_removed`, `l3.default_route_changed`,
`metric.anomaly` - rendered its Arabic human name correctly next to the
unchanged technical code, read naturally, no leftover English word order.
The Diagnostics "events by family" card showed all 9 families present in
this recording (`change` produced nothing, consistent with it being
listed as a reserved/not-yet-produced family) each with a correct Arabic
name beside the code.

Also caught something that was not a real bug: a long-lived browser tab
left open across this whole multi-hour session threw
`ReferenceError: backoffMs is not defined` and a "Rendered more hooks
than during the previous render" error on reload - `backoffMs` does not
exist anywhere in current source (`grep -rn backoffMs src/` - no
matches), and it was a variable name from before Phase 3's useRecord.ts
rewrite. Restarting the dev server process did not clear it; closing the
tab and opening a fresh one against the same server did, confirming it
was that tab's own stale module state, not a regression - recorded here
rather than silently ignored, per this execution order's rule against
dismissing an observed error without checking it against the actual
source first.

## 7. What this slice does not touch (still open in ADR 0004)

Rust/Go structured error codes for capability `reason` and shell
connection failures; `desktop/src/i18n/generated/rules.json` (so demo/
bundle mode get the rule-level Arabic already built in the first Phase 4
slice); `Rules.tsx` and the rest of `IncidentCard.tsx`
(`incident.title`/`link.why`/`incident.advice`) still render the raw
English rule text directly - visible in this same session's screenshots,
where the kind labels are Arabic but the incident title and advice
sentences are still English; the automated "no untagged Latin sentence"
scanner. All tracked in ADR 0004, not folded into "done" by this slice.
